{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,280p' scripts/jobs/ensure-project-worktree.sh; gh pr view 1088 --repo endojs/endo-but-for-bots --json url,state,isDraft,headRefName,headRepositoryOwner,baseRefName,headRefOid,body,title,author\"","aggregated_output":"{\"author\":{\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"is_bot\":false,\"login\":\"kriscendobot\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\"},\"baseRefName\":\"llm\",\"body\":\"Standing pull request for the continuous **Ironhorse fuzz** campaign: each\\nreproduced finding amends this one PR with a load-bearing regression case and\\nits causal fix. Base `llm`. Adopted by the durable marker below.\\n\\n<!-- garden-job: ironhorse-fuzz-findings -->\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Finding `493390fc03979205` — target `differential_regexp_surface`\\n\\n**Symptom.** The 4-byte minimized input `08 74 74 2a` (sha256\\n`450a95b7…dabbcf7`) folds, via the structure-aware generator, into a deeply\\nnested `new RegExp(<pattern>, \\\"s\\\").toString()` whose rendered source is **1099\\nbytes**. The target panicked with a *result* divergence vs the XS pin.\\n\\n**Root cause (oracle-side, not a port defect).** The XS differential oracle\\ncaptured the completion value into a fixed **1024-byte** buffer\\n(`ENDOR_RESULT_MAX`) and `strncpy`-truncated it to 1023 bytes. The port\\n(`ironhorse`) rendered the whole 1099-byte string **correctly**, so the harness\\ncompared the oracle's truncated 1023-byte prefix against the port's full result\\nand reported a false divergence. Any program whose completion value exceeds the\\nbuffer was a latent false positive of this class.\\n\\n**Fix (minimal, oracle-side).**\\n- Grow `ENDOR_RESULT_MAX` to **16 KiB** so realistic completion values compare\\n  in full instead of at a 1 KiB cliff.\\n- Record the untruncated byte length (`result_len`) at both capture sites and\\n  surface `OracleOutcome::result_truncated`. When a value still overflows the\\n  buffer, `differential_check_with_symbols` / `differential_check_result_only`\\n  **skip honestly** (like an out-of-subset program) instead of comparing a\\n  truncated prefix — so the class can never spuriously diverge again, at any\\n  size.\\n\\n**Regressions.**\\n- `ironhorse-fuzz`: replays the exact 4-byte input through the real differential\\n  check and asserts **no divergence** (load-bearing — fails if the oracle fix is\\n  reverted).\\n- `xs-oracle`: a 2000-char completion value is captured **untruncated** and not\\n  flagged truncated.\\n- `ironhorse-vm`: replays the finding's program as frozen bytecode+symbols\\n  (**no XS oracle / no `c/moddable` submodule**) and asserts the port renders\\n  the whole 1099-byte result without panicking or truncating.\\n\\n**Note (latent twin).** `ModuleRunOutcome` shares the same fixed result buffer;\\nthe C side now records `result_len` on that path too, but the module differential\\ntargets do not yet consult a `result_truncated` flag. Out of scope for this\\nfinding; worth a follow-up if a module fuzz target ever surfaces a >16 KiB result.\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Finding `d99d263fcf6ca7a7` — target `differential_source`\\n\\n**Symptom.** The 5-byte minimized input `2d 57 27 48 86` (sha256\\n`749f2021…e09a4f9`) folds, via the structure-aware generator, into the\\narithmetic program\\n\\n```js\\n((729808896 && (729808896 && (-83 && 327155712)))\\n * (((-83 && 327155712) * (729808896 % 603979776)) % 729808896))\\n```\\n\\nThe target panicked with a *result* divergence:\\n`oracle=\\\"57632001481506816\\\"` vs `ironhorse=\\\"57632001481506820\\\"`.\\n\\n**Root cause (harness-side, not a port defect).** In IEEE-754 doubles\\n(ECMAScript Number semantics) the program evaluates to\\n`327155712 * 176160768` = `819 · 2^46`, the **exactly-representable** double\\nwhose real value is `57632001481506816`. Both engines compute the *identical*\\ndouble — they differ only in how they render it to a string:\\n\\n- XS's `fx_dtoa` (xsdtoa.c) prints the double's exact